<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=cursus-publicus">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cursus publicus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The cursus publicus was the state courier and transport service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, established by Augustus, with relay stations along the main roads. It carried official messages, officials and goods. The system continued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, supporting imperial communication and administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cursus-publicus.jpg" alt="Cursus publicus" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abraham Ortelius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tabula_Itineraria_Ex_Illustri_Peutingerorum_Biblitheca_Quae_Augustae_Vindel._Est_Beneficio_Marci_Velseri_Septemviri_Augustani_In_Lucem_Edita.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> adapted their state post from the Persian network of royal mounted couriers described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, in which letters passed from rider to rider along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a>. A courier service may have existed under the Republic, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> states clearly that Augustus created the system, to carry messages, officials and tax revenues between the provinces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Augustus first stationed young men at short intervals along the military roads and then switched to vehicles, so that one man made the whole journey and could be questioned on arrival.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The service rested on obligations placed on private persons. Contractors called mancipes supplied the equipment, animals and wagons, and the stations were maintained from the resources of the areas the roads crossed; in Late Antiquity the cost was charged to provincial taxpayers. Along the main roads stood change stations with repair facilities, the mutationes, and full stations with lodging, the mansiones. Use required a diploma naming the holder, the period, the route and the lodgings, and governors and minor officials abused these to travel free.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> a praefectus vehiculorum, Lucius Volusius Maecianus, supervised the service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, while governors managed it in the provinces. After the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> it had two sections, the fast cursus velox with horses and mules and the regular cursus clabularis with oxen for heavy goods. Julian, emperor from 361 to 363, tried to restrict the granting of passes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the Jerusalem Itinerary the night stations appear to have been about twenty-five Roman miles apart, and a typical day's journey was 38 to 62 miles, though a single rider could cover 160 km or more in an emergency. News of the accession of Pertinax on 1 January 193 was announced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 6 March.</p><p>The service was still fully working in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Eastern Empire</a> in the first half of the 6th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> accused Justinian of dismantling most of it except the route to the Persian border. The dromos continued through the Byzantine period, much reduced, under the logothetēs tou dromou, and by the 11th century its upkeep fell to local peasants. In the West it survived under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a>'s Ostrogoths in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cursus publicus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It governed from the imperial palace through a network of themes and provincial centres, whose officials reported to Constantinople through chains of command kept working across great distances by the state post, the <b>cursus publicus</b>, known in Greek as the dromos.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>late 1st century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Rome</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/augustus-roman-emperor">Augustus (Roman emperor)</a> · <a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The article allows that a courier service may have existed for a time under the Roman Republic, but says the clearest reference, by Suetonius, states that Augustus created the system.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road and relay posts<span>c. 500 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=cursus-publicus">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
